(K(1,1,r)) \\cup E(K(1,1,r)) \\to \\{1, 2, \\ldots, 3r + 3\\}\\) as follows:\n\n\\(f(u_1) = 1,\\ f(u_2) = 3r + 3,\\ f(v_i) = 3i + 1\\) for \\(1 \\le i \\le r\\); and\n\n\\(f(u_1u_2) = 3r + 2,\\ f(u_1v_i) = 3i,\\ f(u_2v_i) = 3(r - i) + 2\\) for \\(1 \\le i \\le r\\).\n\nIt is easy to verify that \\(f\\) is a bijection with \\(f(uv) = |f(u) - f(v)|\\) for every edge \\(uv \\in E(K(1,1,r))\\). Hence, \\(K(1,1,r)\\) is super graceful. ■\n\n**Example 2.1.** In Fig. 1, we give the super graceful labeling of \\(K(1,1,5)\\) according to the function defined above.\n\n**Problem 2.1.** Study the super gracefulness of complete tripartite graph \\(K(p, q, r)\\), \\(r \\ge q \\ge p \\ge 1, q \\ge 2\\).\n\n**Definition 2.1.** Let \\(K(1,n_1) \\cup K(1,n_2) \\cup \\cdots \\cup K(1,n_m)\\) be the disjoint union of \\(m\\) copies of star graphs \\(K(1,n_i)\\) for \\(n_i \\ge 1\\) and \\(1 \\le i \\le m\\).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn \\(k\\)-super gracefulness of some complete multi-partite graphs (https://combinatorialpress.com/jcmcc-articles/volume-131/on-k-super-gracefulness-of-some-complete-multi-partite-graphs/)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Conjecture 3.6. For \\(t\\ge 3\\), a complete \\(t\\)-partite graph \\(G\\) is \\(k\\)-super graceful if and only if \\(G=K(1,m,r)\\) and \\(k=1\\). ... The approach can be extended to obtained infinitely many \\(k\\)-super graceful bipartite graphs.\n\\(\\square\\)\n\nIn [8, Theorem 4.6], the authors showed that \\(K(1,m,r)\\) is super graceful for \\(m,r\\ge 1\\).\n\nConjecture 3.6. For \\(t\\ge 3\\), a complete \\(t\\)-partite graph \\(G\\) is \\(k\\)-super graceful if and only if \\(G=K(1,m,r)\\) and \\(k=1\\).\n\nIn [8, Construction C4], the authors gave a way to construct infinitely many super graceful bipartite graphs. The approach can be extended to obtained infinitely many \\(k\\)-super graceful bipartite graphs.\n\nApproach A1. Begin with vertices